</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LitBank.</span>
We compare the proposed methods with baselines on LitBank (see Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2 Results Comparison ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ImCoref-CeS: An Improved Lightweight Pipeline for Coreference Resolution with LLM-based Checker-Splitter Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), with full results available in Table <a href="#A6.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Appendix F Additional Experimental Results ‣ ImCoref-CeS: An Improved Lightweight Pipeline for Coreference Resolution with LLM-based Checker-Splitter Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> of Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Additional Experimental Results ‣ ImCoref-CeS: An Improved Lightweight Pipeline for Coreference Resolution with LLM-based Checker-Splitter Refinement" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
Specifically, in terms of Avg.F1, ImCoref-CeS consistently outperforms all baselines apart from LLMLink, with ImCoref-CeS<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{dpsk}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>dpsk</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{dpsk}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieving the highest performance. This further validates the superior performance of our method. Notably, although LLMLink attains second-best coreference performance, it requires training multiple LLMs, incurring substantially higher computational costs.
Among supervised neural methods, ImCoref achieves the best Avg. F1 (79.7%), surpassing Maverick<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{mes}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>mes</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{mes}}</annotation></semantics></math> (78.4%) and Dual-cache (79.5%). However, ImCoref ranks second in the MUC metric (87.9%), slightly behind longdoc and Dual-cache (both 88.2%). This suggests that while our method excels overall, there remains room for improvement in MUC compared to models specifically designed for long-text coreference. We plan to address this in future work by exploring more suitable mention clustering modules.</p>
</div>
